  "final_span": "the table with a toffee hammer.\nAfter 33 minutes had passed, Montreal gained a corner kick. The large 6-7 Drogba maneuvered to the forefront to powerfully header the ball backfield. Bernier, though small in build, found himself on the floor and managed to stand up using just one leg. The tiny crowd exploded with joy.\nBernier struck the ball powerfully with two more hard headers as Montreal intensified their attack. He reached the second with mere moments left before halftime.\n\"Our pattern of play changed a little bit,\" Biello conceded. \"I don't know if I instructed guys to go away from the centre, but guys on the bench went away from the centre so right before the whistle Bernier was kind of isolated out front\u2026 We made a change right before the whistle hoping somebody would slide away from the centre.\"\nApplause marked the goal, yet the playing surface was intolerable. Nearby street-hockey players observed with sinews as elastic as gypsies'.\nNone appeared to suggest a game at Saputo Stadium was as suited for snowball fights as it had been on that particular day.\nA field covered in sand restricts the incoming ball from caroming freely. Within the sixth minute, Piatti delivered the final pass that Bernier turned into a penalty, setting a Canadian MLS playoff record.\nAndover Jordan disrupted the Impact's goal-scoring chance, as the big Belgian defender Hasain El-Khdred's effort sailed wide. Didier missed.\nVanney, clearly flustered, substituted Bradley after some time, inserting Will Johnson into the game in his place. Yet TFC was too far behind to make any difference, losing to an in-form Impact team and showcasing significant creativity and skill.\nThe objective is to guide Montreal's low-scoring periods by closing Giivinco off from the straightaway and pressuring him near the penalty box continuously. The primary obstacle was provided by midfielder Patrice Bernier, who occupied key areas, and occasionally thwarted progress, touching the ball infrequently.\nToronto's front-runners, Giovinco and Altidore, commenced to channel the power of heavy marksmen",
